<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  SOLO'S SCOPE IS A USER OPTION, AND `global` IS THE DEFAULT (QBX-256).

  THIS CASE WRITES the per-user key `mixer/soloScope` and is RUN_SERIAL for
  that reason (smaragd/CMakeLists.txt says so beside mixer_sections, which
  owns `mixer/sections` the same way). It restores the SOpt default, `global`,
  at the end, so the shared smaragd.ini comes back with identical content.
  `track_detail_meter_solo_scope.qxa` writes the key too, because QBX-233's
  claim — which root is the rule asked about? — exists only in the per-root
  scope; it is RUN_SERIAL and restores the default as well. Both headers and
  the CMakeLists entry say so.

  THE SHAPE, and why it is this one. Fixture test_sawtooth.wav, 4 s, amplitude
  ramping 0 to 0.8, so sec0 of ONE copy measures RMS 0.06665 (measured, and
  the same number arrangement_edit_audible.qxa is built on). Copies at the
  same position are coherent, so n copies measure n x 0.06665.

    master lane 0   the ONLY placement of the "Drums" arrangement, at 0
    master lane 1   one copy of its own, at 0, at -6 dB (x0.50119)
    Drums:0         one copy at 0          <- the "bass drum"
    Drums:1         one copy at 0          <- the rest of the loop
    Unused:0        one copy at 0, in an arrangement NOTHING PLACES

  The -6 dB is what makes the case readable: without it "the loop alone" and
  "the master lane alone" are both one copy and the same number, so a wrong
  answer could pass as a right one. With it every stage below has its own
  closed form, and no two are within 25 % of each other:

    everything audible            2 + 0.50119 = 2.50119 copies   0.16671
    Drums:0 soloed, global        1                              0.06665
    master lane 1 soloed, global  0.50119                        0.03341
    master lane 0 soloed, global  2 (the loop IN FULL)           0.13330
    Drums:0 soloed, arrangement   1 + 0.50119 = 1.50119          0.10006
    Unused:0 soloed, global       nothing                        silence
    carrier's own clip added,
      Drums:0 soloed, global      1 (the carrier's clip muted)   0.06665
      the same, solo released     2.50119 + 1 = 3.50119          0.23335

  THE FOUR CLAIMS, one per stage, and each is a different term of the rule:

  1. `global` + a solo INSIDE an arrangement silences the master's own lanes
     too (stage B). This is the behaviour the option exists for.
  2. `global` + a solo on the master lane that CARRIES the arrangement plays
     the loop IN FULL (stage D), not silence, and the lanes inside it report
     themselves AUDIBLE (stage H). This is the trap the "inside a soloed
     ancestor" term exists for: a project-wide anySolo is true inside the
     arrangement as well, where nothing is soloed, so without that term every
     lane of the loop answers inaudible.

     MEASURED, AND IT IS WHY THERE ARE TWO STAGES. Removing the term alone
     leaves stage D's number UNCHANGED at 0.13330: the asset capture is not
     rebuilt when an arrangement's audibility changes for a reason OUTSIDE
     that arrangement, so the placement goes on playing the mix the capture
     already holds. In this build that is unobservable — the term's answer is
     exactly what the capture contains — but it means no render in this
     fixture can see the term. Stage H asks the RULE instead, through
     describeTrackDetailDock's `audible=`, and measures 0 with the term
     removed against 1 with it. D keeps its place as the claim that the loop
     is not silenced; H is what makes the claim load-bearing.
  3. `arrangement` reproduces the pre-option behaviour exactly (stage E): the
     loop drops to its soloed lane and the master's own lane keeps playing.
  4. A solo in an arrangement that is PLACED NOWHERE silences the project
     (stage F). That is the honest consequence of a project-wide rule rather
     than a special case, and it is also why QBX-257 puts an indicator in the
     status bar: the cause is in a tab the user may not be looking at.
  5. A solo DOES NOT LEAK through the carrier lane (stage G). The lane that
     holds the placement must stay audible, or the path to the soloed lane is
     cut — but the clips it holds of its OWN are not what the user soloed, and
     before the clip-entry half of the rule they played: measured 0.1333 where
     the soloed lane alone is 0.06665. G2 is its control, because a clip muted
     and never unmuted would pass G.

  Stage A is the control: with the option set and nothing soloed, every stage's
  arithmetic is the same arithmetic.

  WHAT EACH STAGE DISCRIMINATES AGAINST, measured rather than assumed. Against
  the PRE-OPTION build (main, which ignores the key and is per-root), B
  measures 0.100054 and F measures 0.166704, so those two fail there and are
  the gate's credential. A, C, C2, D and E pass on that build as well, and
  that is deliberate: C2/D/E are answers the two scopes SHARE, and D's claim
  is not about the pre-option build at all. D discriminates against a GLOBAL
  build that lost the "inside a soloed ancestor" term, where it measures 0 —
  which is what the sabotage arm for that term shows.

  `assert-mixing-options` pins the ROUND TRIP through the real Options dialog
  (the INI value is what the page shows, so a user opening Options after this
  case does not see a stale default and write it back). It does NOT pin
  SOpt::def() — the case always writes the key explicitly, because a case
  that depended on the INI's prior state would pass or fail by what ran before
  it. The default lives in soptions.cpp beside every other one.
-->
<SActionScript version="1" name="solo_scope_project_wide">

  <setup project="new"/>

  <actions>
    <set-option key="mixer/soloScope" value="global"/>
    <assert-mixing-options soloScope="global"/>

    <add-track index="-1"/>                 <!-- 0: carries the asset -->
    <add-track index="-1"/>                 <!-- 1: its own copy, -6 dB -->
    <add-sample trackPath="1" filePath="../test_sawtooth.wav" timePos="0"/>
    <set-track-volume trackPath="1" volume="-6"/>

    <create-arrangement name="Drums"/>
    <add-track arrangement="Drums" index="-1"/>
    <add-track arrangement="Drums" index="-1"/>
    <add-sample trackPath="Drums:0" filePath="../test_sawtooth.wav" timePos="0"/>
    <add-sample trackPath="Drums:1" filePath="../test_sawtooth.wav" timePos="0"/>
    <assert-track-count arrangement="Drums" count="2"/>

    <create-asset container="Drums:" startOffset="0" duration="192000"
                  assetName="DrumsAsset"/>
    <place-asset assetName="DrumsAsset" trackPath="0" timePos="0"/>

    <!-- A: the control. -->
    <render filename="solo_scope_A.wav" format="wav" quality="10" durationSec="4"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="solo_scope_A.wav"
                         startFrame="0" frameCount="48000"
                         minRms="0.1617" maxRms="0.1717"/>

    <!-- B: claim 1. A solo INSIDE the arrangement is in force everywhere. -->
    <set-track-solo trackPath="Drums:0" solo="1"/>
    <render filename="solo_scope_B.wav" format="wav" quality="10" durationSec="4"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="solo_scope_B.wav"
                         startFrame="0" frameCount="48000"
                         minRms="0.0647" maxRms="0.0687"/>

    <!-- C: released, and back to the control's own number. -->
    <set-track-solo trackPath="Drums:0" solo="0"/>
    <render filename="solo_scope_C.wav" format="wav" quality="10" durationSec="4"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="solo_scope_C.wav"
                         startFrame="0" frameCount="48000"
                         minRms="0.1617" maxRms="0.1717"/>

    <!-- C2: a MASTER lane soloed, the one that carries nothing of the loop.
         The loop goes silent, which is the rule working in the other
         direction. -->
    <set-track-solo trackPath="1" solo="1"/>
    <render filename="solo_scope_C2.wav" format="wav" quality="10" durationSec="4"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="solo_scope_C2.wav"
                         startFrame="0" frameCount="48000"
                         minRms="0.0324" maxRms="0.0344"/>
    <set-track-solo trackPath="1" solo="0"/>

    <!-- D: claim 2. The CARRIER lane soloed plays the loop IN FULL. -->
    <set-track-solo trackPath="0" solo="1"/>
    <render filename="solo_scope_D.wav" format="wav" quality="10" durationSec="4"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="solo_scope_D.wav"
                         startFrame="0" frameCount="48000"
                         minRms="0.1293" maxRms="0.1373"/>
    <set-track-solo trackPath="0" solo="0"/>

    <!-- E: claim 3. The other scope, the pre-option behaviour. -->
    <set-option key="mixer/soloScope" value="arrangement"/>
    <assert-mixing-options soloScope="arrangement"/>
    <set-track-solo trackPath="Drums:0" solo="1"/>
    <render filename="solo_scope_E.wav" format="wav" quality="10" durationSec="4"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="solo_scope_E.wav"
                         startFrame="0" frameCount="48000"
                         minRms="0.0971" maxRms="0.1031"/>
    <set-track-solo trackPath="Drums:0" solo="0"/>

    <!-- F: claim 4. A solo in an arrangement nothing places. -->
    <set-option key="mixer/soloScope" value="global"/>
    <create-arrangement name="Unused"/>
    <add-track arrangement="Unused" index="-1"/>
    <add-sample trackPath="Unused:0" filePath="../test_sawtooth.wav" timePos="0"/>
    <set-track-solo trackPath="Unused:0" solo="1"/>
    <render filename="solo_scope_F.wav" format="wav" quality="10" durationSec="4"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="solo_scope_F.wav"
                         startFrame="0" frameCount="48000"
                         minRms="0.0" maxRms="0.001"/>
    <set-track-solo trackPath="Unused:0" solo="0"/>

    <!-- G: claim 5. The CARRIER lane keeps its plug so the soloed lane can
         be heard through it, and its OWN clip is muted while that is the only
         reason it is audible. G2 releases the solo and proves the mute came
         back off: a clip muted for ever would satisfy G just as well. -->
    <add-sample trackPath="0" filePath="../test_sawtooth.wav" timePos="0"/>
    <set-track-solo trackPath="Drums:0" solo="1"/>
    <render filename="solo_scope_G.wav" format="wav" quality="10" durationSec="4"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="solo_scope_G.wav"
                         startFrame="0" frameCount="48000"
                         minRms="0.0647" maxRms="0.0687"/>
    <set-track-solo trackPath="Drums:0" solo="0"/>
    <render filename="solo_scope_G2.wav" format="wav" quality="10" durationSec="4"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="solo_scope_G2.wav"
                         startFrame="0" frameCount="48000"
                         minRms="0.2264" maxRms="0.2403"/>

    <!-- H: claim 2's REAL discriminator. See the header: stage D's number
         cannot see the "inside a soloed ancestor" term, so the term is pinned
         on the rule itself, through the one seam that reports it
         (describeTrackDetailDock's `audible=`, which calls the same
         meterAudible() the meter calls). Last in the case, because the tab
         switch below changes what a view GESTURE like select-track means. -->
    <assert-track-detail-dock contains="|rows="/>
    <open-arrangement-tab name="Drums"/>
    <activate-tab name="Drums"/>
    <set-track-solo trackPath="0" solo="1"/>
    <select-track trackPath="0"/>
    <assert-track-detail-dock contains="|path=Drums:0"/>
    <assert-track-detail-dock contains="|audible=1"/>
    <set-track-solo trackPath="0" solo="0"/>

    <!-- The INI comes back as it was: `global` is SOpt::def(). -->
    <set-option key="mixer/soloScope" value="global"/>
    <assert-mixing-options soloScope="global"/>
  </actions>

  <assertions>
    <assert-track-count equals="2"/>
  </assertions>

</SActionScript>
